The historical Alembic chain upgrades existing initialized installations. Its
original root assumes the former schema already exists; it is not a fresh
PostgreSQL provisioning baseline. Historical revisions are self-contained and
must not import deleted application modules or contact bot providers.

Fresh PostgreSQL deployment initialization is a separate, explicit command:

    python -m scripts.initialize_registry_database

It refuses any existing application table, including alembic_version. It creates
only the current Registry ORM schema and Store/UOM seed, then records the current
Alembic head using the same connection and transaction. It executes no historical
upgrade function and creates no bots or credentials. Staff provisioning remains
an explicit separate operation.

Isolated Codespaces databases use scripts.security_bootstrap to create the
current ORM schema directly. Fresh initialization creates a default Store,
Store-owned units and administrator grants. It never registers bots or creates
provider credentials. Do not stamp or replay historical revisions over it.

Explicit retirement
-------------------

Back up existing data before retiring obsolete tables.

For an unversioned, isolated Codespaces database, inspect the planned cleanup:

    python -m scripts.legacy_schema_cleanup

Then apply the bounded cleanup explicitly:

    python -m scripts.legacy_schema_cleanup --apply

For an existing versioned installation, use the guarded terminal revision:

    alembic -x retire_original_schema=true upgrade head

The fixed allowlist contains the original single-bot tables and obsolete global
control tables. Cleanup rejects external foreign keys and uses transactional
PostgreSQL DROP RESTRICT. Staff/MFA, Stores, modern catalog/orders, canonical
Store Customers and historical Bot Registry owners remain intact. Singleton
merchant text moves into default Store settings; conflicting existing text
blocks cleanup. Retired identity links must already be represented by shared
Store Customer ownership. Historical workflow bridges are disabled and fallback
values normalized to ignore; bindings and their referenced Runs remain intact.

Cleanup does not recreate bots, read environment-owned provider tokens, delete
modern bot-owned commerce rows or invent a downgrade for deleted history.
